  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Dry weather will prevail today through Sunday \nmorning before the chance of showers increases Sunday afternoon and \nevening.\n\nSurface high pressure over the eastern Great Lakes early this \nmorning will bring a cool start to the day following a night of \nstrong radiational cooling. Expect some patchy fog through early to \nmid morning, mainly across the river valleys of the Southern\nTier. Otherwise, expect mostly sunny skies today with a few \npatches of mid/high clouds at times. Temperatures will warm to \nwithin a degree or two of average this afternoon following the \ncool start to the day.\n\nDry weather will continue tonight with increasing clouds from west \nto east as a warm front develops upstream across the central Great \nLakes. Some patchy fog may develop overnight across the river \nvalleys of the Southern Tier.\n\nSunday will start dry with any showers remaining north of the area \non the Canadian side of the border. Latest model guidance continues \nto trend faster and farther south with the arrival of the first rain \nchances Sunday afternoon, necessitating an increase in POPS. A ridge \nwill continue to build over the central US, while a sharp mid level \ntrough digs southeast across Quebec. The interaction of these two \nfeatures will result in thermal packing across the Great Lakes, \nallowing a WNW to ESE oriented warm front to develop and strengthen \nfrom the upper Great Lakes to NY by Sunday afternoon and evening.\n\nThe stronger frontogenetic forcing and moisture transport will be \nfound across the upper Great Lakes Sunday afternoon and evening, but \nenough moisture and weak forcing will spread into the eastern Great \nLakes to support a chance of a few showers by afternoon.  \n\nKey Message 2...The pattern will become unsettled Sunday night \nthrough the middle of next week with increasing humidity and several \nrounds of showers and thunderstorms.  \n\nMonday through Tuesday, a strong/hot ridge will remain in place \nacross much of the Central US and extending into the lower Ohio \nValley. Meanwhile, a trough will persist across Quebec. A frontal \nzone with a seasonably strong thermal gradient will remain in place \nfrom the upper Great Lakes to NY/PA through early next week, \nmeandering north and south at times with passing shortwaves. \nClusters of showers and thunderstorms and their associated cold \npools may also modulate the frontal position at times.\n\nThe periodic forcing from passing shortwaves along with persistent \nmoisture convergence along the frontal zone will support several \nrounds of showers and scattered thunderstorms Sunday night through \nTuesday. Instability will likely be limited during this period, \nkeeping the severe risk low.\n\nThe pattern may become more conducive to severe weather potential by \nWednesday as a strong Pacific shortwave moves east across southern \nCanada. An EML plume is forecast to be advected northeastward across \nthe Ohio Valley and may reach the eastern Great Lakes by Wednesday. \nThis feature will combine with an underlying warm/humid airmass to \nsupport the potential for moderate to strong instability as the \nshortwave passes by to the north and resultant flow adjustments \nbring increasing shear profiles. The severe weather potential for \nWednesday will continue to be monitored in the coming days as the \ndetails become more clear.",
